        - # English grapheme-to-phoneme, ported from misaki (`misaki/en.py`), which is the
        - # G2P Kokoro was trained against.
        - #
        - # ## Why this is here rather than shelled out to Python
        - #
        - # Kokoro does not consume text. It consumes a phoneme string in misaki's alphabet
        - # — `hˈEllO wˈɜɹld` — which its `vocab` maps to embedding ids. A runner that
        - # cannot produce that string is a runner that cannot be called with a sentence,
        - # and pointing it at a Python install turns "load a package and speak" into "set
        - # up a virtualenv, then speak".
        - #
        - # ## What is ported, and what a missing part-of-speech tagger costs
        - #
        - # misaki tags every token with spaCy and uses the tag in four places: heteronyms
        - # (`read`, `lead`, `live`, `wind`), proper-noun spelling-out, `a` vs `A`, and a
        - # handful of function words. There is no tagger here, so those take the lexicon's
        - # `DEFAULT`.
        - #
        - # **That is the only approximation.** Everything downstream of the tag is a
        - # faithful port: `apply_stress` (including the `restress` reordering),
        - # `is_known`, `lookup`, the three stemmers with their voicing rules, `get_NNP`,
        - # and the right-to-left `future_vowel` context that decides `the` -> `ðə`/`ði`
        - # and `to` -> `tə`/`tʊ`. Those four are context, not tags, so they are exact.
